r-speed, higher-height, repeated, or reactive plyometric work.","Prescribe low-repetition quality sets, landing contacts per side, approach speed or height, hold duration, rest, cue complexity, and progression criteria; stop before fatigue changes mechanics."]},{"sectionKey":"instructions","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6208302/","sourceTitle":"The Use of Augmented Information for Reducing Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injury Risk During Jump Landings: A Systematic Review","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Athletic Training","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":87,"claims":["Prescriptive instructions and feedback can improve landing mechanics, while excessive or poorly targeted information can interfere with learning.","Use a small cue set: land quietly, share force through ankle-knee-hip flexion, keep knee over the middle toes, control pelvis and trunk, and hold the finish without a touch-down or extra step."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/downloadnsa?filename=8c33cc0b-ba23-4d3d-9dbe-168e10d5fcfb.pdf&urlslug=plyometrics-for-beginners-basic-considerati","sourceTitle":"Plyometrics for Beginners: Basic Considerations","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":76,"claims":["Loud contact, poor stabilization, loss of alignment, or inability to use the ankle, knee, and hip together indicate that intensity or volume exceeds current control.","Stop for pain, giving way, dizziness, repeated valgus or trunk collapse, uncontrolled foot contact, extra steps, loss of the required hold, unsafe surface or spacing, or persistent left-right asymmetry."]},{"sectionKey":"programming","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6592422/","sourceTitle":"Evidence-Based Best-Practice Guidelines for Preventing Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injuries in Young Female Athletes: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis","sourcePublisher":"The American Journal of